.TH QTTEST 1
.SH NAME
qttest \- test quicktime(2)'s sample tables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qttest
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Qttest
parses
.B /lib/movies/test.mov
with
.IR quicktime (2)
and checks the result against what that file is known to contain: one
video track,
.BR avc1 ,
64 by 64, ten samples.
Given a
.I file
argument it parses that instead and checks only what holds for any
movie.
.PP
Checking against a fixture with known contents is the point.
A parser returning plausible rubbish \(em one track, some samples \(em
passes a smoke test and fails this.
.PP
It also requires that every sample lie inside the file and be
non-empty, that every duration be positive, that the first sample be a
sync sample (nothing can start decoding otherwise), and that the track
carry
.B avcC
setup data with a sane configuration version, since without it the
samples cannot be given to a decoder at all.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/qttest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR quicktime (2),
.IR draw (3)
.SH BUGS
The fixture is one short, single-track, all-intra-plus-P-frame H.264
movie written by one encoder.
Multi-track files, 64-bit chunk offsets
.RB ( co64 ),
uniform sample sizes, edit lists and audio tracks are not exercised,
though the code paths for several of those exist.
.PP
Error handling is checked by hand rather than here: a JPEG, a truncated
movie, an empty file and random bytes each produce a diagnostic rather
than a crash.
